Subject: [PATCH 30/30] TODO 13.3(E): two of its three rows were testable after all
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

13.3(E) listed three things "unverified by construction". Re-read, two of
them were verified-by-nobody rather than unverifiable.

1. "_eword()'s 'word not in the dictionary' branch is not watched to
   fire. It is reachable only if `evaluate` itself is missing, which
   cannot happen in a firmware that has reached the prompt."

   True of the CALLERS and false of the branch: eword() takes the word to
   run as an ARGUMENT, so any name that does not exist reaches it.
   `test-eword-report` asks for one. The distinction is worth a fixture
   because the two failures are not the same failure -- a -1 throw means
   the word ran and aborted; this means NOTHING RAN -- and before the
   reporter existed both left ret == -1 and printed the same nothing.

2. "Untested printf surface: %n, and long long conversions beyond the
   %8.8lx shape the tree actually uses."

   Both are implemented (libc/vsprintf.c's case 'n', and the 'L'
   qualifier that `ll` also maps to) and neither had ever been run. An
   implemented-but-unrun conversion is an UNKNOWN, not a pass. Two cases
   join test-printf-edges, taking it from 12/12 to 14/14:

     pct-n   "ab%ncd" -> abcd, rc 4, n 2
     llx     %llx of 0x123456789abcdef -> 123456789abcdef, rc 15

   %n IS the return value, written through a pointer at the point it
   appears -- so it asserts the same quantity the trunc cases assert
   about rc, at a different MOMENT: mid-format rather than at the end.
   And the llx case needs 60 bits, so on x86 it prints a number the
   Forth stack under it cannot hold.

The third row stands and is re-derived rather than re-quoted: sparc32 and
sparc64 still carry amd64's `preopen` omission -- measured 2026-08-27,
`device-end` appears 0 times in each, against 1 in x86 and 3 in amd64 --
and this lab still cannot boot sparc, so it stays UNVERIFIED and
check-patch-scope.sh still prints NOT COVERED: sparc on every run.

WATCHED TO BITE, all three at once, each naming its own defect:

  * the printk in _eword's not-found branch removed -> "test-eword-report
    produced 0 lines, expected exactly 1"
  * case 'n' storing 0 instead of str-buf -> "pct-n ... n=0 MISMATCH"
  * the 'L' qualifier reading unsigned int -> "llx ... got[89abcdef](8)
    MISMATCH", which is the low half of the value and the same shape as
    13.2(b) one layer down

with the ratio dropping to 12/14 to catch them in aggregate.

diff --git a/libopenbios/init.c b/libopenbios/init.c
--- a/libopenbios/init.c
+++ b/libopenbios/init.c
@@ -89,6 +89,27 @@
 	(void)feval("openbios-feval-selftest-no-such-word");
 }
 
+/* THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME REPORTER, and TODO 13.3(E) listed it as
+ * unverified by construction: "_eword()'s 'word not in the dictionary' branch
+ * is not watched to fire. It is reachable only if `evaluate` itself is
+ * missing, which cannot happen in a firmware that has reached the prompt."
+ *
+ * That is true of the CALLERS and false of the branch. eword() takes the word
+ * to run as an argument, so the branch is reachable by asking for any word that
+ * does not exist -- which is what this does. The distinction matters because
+ * the two failures are not the same failure: a -1 THROW means the word ran and
+ * aborted, while this means NOTHING RAN, and before the reporter existed both
+ * left ret == -1 and printed the same nothing.
+ *
+ * Leaves the stack as it found it, like the feval fixture: nargs is 0, so
+ * _eword has nothing to unwind even on the path that does run something.
+ */
+static void eword_report_selftest( void )
+{
+	static xt_t never_cached = 0;
+	(void)_eword("openbios-eword-selftest-no-such-word", &never_cached, 0);
+}
+
 /* THE PRECISION FIX'S OWN FIXTURE SET, run inside the shipped firmware.
  *
  * libc/vsprintf.c's %s case treated `precision` as a MINIMUM. Six of the seven
@@ -365,6 +386,47 @@
 		       got, (int)strlen(got), rc);
 	}
 
+	/* TODO 13.3(E)'s "untested printf surface": %n, and a long long wider
+	 * than a cell. Both are implemented (libc/vsprintf.c case 'n' and the
+	 * 'L' qualifier, which `ll` also maps to) and neither had ever been
+	 * exercised -- an implemented-but-unrun conversion is an UNKNOWN, not a
+	 * pass.
+	 *
+	 * %n IS the return value, written through a pointer: it stores
+	 * str - buf at the point it appears. So this case asserts the same
+	 * quantity the trunc cases assert about rc, at a DIFFERENT moment --
+	 * mid-format rather than at the end -- which is the only way to catch a
+	 * str that is advanced correctly overall but not while running. */
+	{
+		int nn = -1;
+		n++;
+		rc = snprintf(got, sizeof(got), "ab%ncd", &nn);
+		if( nn == 2 && rc == 4 && !strcmp(got, "abcd") ) {
+			ok++;
+			printk("printf-edges: pct-n want[abcd](4) n=2"
+			       " got[%s](%d) n=%d OK\n", got, rc, nn);
+		} else {
+			printk("printf-edges: pct-n want[abcd](4) n=2"
+			       " got[%s](%d) n=%d MISMATCH\n", got, rc, nn);
+		}
+	}
+
+	/* A long long WIDER THAN A CELL, which is the case x86 cannot express on
+	 * its stack and can still print: the value below needs 60 bits. If the
+	 * 'L' qualifier were dropped, va_arg would read half of it and the low
+	 * word would come out as the whole number -- the same shape as 13.2(b),
+	 * one layer down. */
+	n++;
+	rc = snprintf(got, sizeof(got), "%llx", 0x123456789abcdefULL);
+	if( !strcmp(got, "123456789abcdef") && rc == 15 ) {
+		ok++;
+		printk("printf-edges: llx want[123456789abcdef](15)"
+		       " got[%s](%d) OK\n", got, rc);
+	} else {
+		printk("printf-edges: llx want[123456789abcdef](15)"
+		       " got[%s](%d) MISMATCH\n", got, rc);
+	}
+
 	printk("printf-edges: %d/%d ok, %d/%d recorded divergence\n",
 	       ok, n, ok_div, n_div);
 }
@@ -390,6 +452,10 @@
 	// The reporter's must-catch fixture -- see feval_report_selftest above
 	bind_func("test-feval-report", feval_report_selftest);
 
+	// ...and eword()'s "not in the dictionary" branch, which is a DIFFERENT
+	// failure from a throw: nothing ran. TODO 13.3(E).
+	bind_func("test-eword-report", eword_report_selftest);
+
 	// libc/vsprintf.c's %s precision fixtures -- see above
 	bind_func("test-printf-precision", printf_precision_selftest);
 
